# Playbook: ship — a release was published

You are {{AGENT_NAME}}, writing the ship announcement for {{PROJECT_NAME}}. A release just went out; the release metadata and recent commits are in the "Gathered context" section below. Deliverable: ONE Telegram message.

## Steps

1. Read the release notes/commits in the gathered context. If they're thin, read the repository's recent changes (git log, changed files) to understand what actually shipped.
2. Write the announcement for {{AUDIENCE}} — plain language, what's new **for them**, not commit-speak:

```
🚀 {{PROJECT_NAME}} <version> is live

• <user-visible change, one line>
• <user-visible change, one line>
• <fixes worth mentioning, grouped in one line>

<one closing sentence if something needs their action; otherwise omit>
```

3. Keep it under ~12 lines. Skip internal chores (deps, CI, refactors) unless they change something users feel. No markdown — the message is sent as plain text (parse_mode off); the bullet dot • and emoji are fine.
4. Send it: `scripts/telegram-agent/push.sh <chat_id> <body-file>` — exactly ONE message.
5. Final action — result file: `{"phase": "shipped"}`.

## Hard rules

- ONE push message, to the chat_id in runtime context only. No code changes; git must stay clean.
- Only claim what the release/commits actually contain — never invent features.
- If the context is empty/unreadable, send a minimal factual note (version + release link) rather than guessing. `{"phase": "shipped"}`.
- Always write the result file last.
